     <p><strong><p><strong>Lydia Schumacher, <em>From Eternal to Everlasting: God and Time in Franciscan Thought</em>, De Gruyter, 2025, 112 pp., $34.99 (pbk) ISBN 9783119149020. </strong></p></strong></p>
     <p><strong>Reviewed by Thomas Williams, Georgetown University</strong></p>
     <p>In contemporary Anglophone discussions of God’s relation to time, the word ‘eternal’ is typically used to describe a divine life in which there is no before and after, no succession, no “flowing now”—nothing, in other words, that could count as temporal. The word ‘everlasting’, by contrast, is used to describe a divine life that does involve temporal succession: a succession that has neither beginning nor end and is accordingly infinite, but succession nonetheless. So, readers coming to Lydia Schumacher’s <em>From Eternal to Everlasting: God and Time in Franciscan Thought</em> may be expecting an argument to the effect that Franciscan thinkers moved away from what had been the standard medieval view that God is timelessly eternal to the view that God is everlasting, having infinite temporal duration.</p>...
